LICENSE NOT CHOSEN YET.

This is deliberate and it is a founder decision, not an oversight.

Most competitions require a public repository under an open source licence.
Choosing one is a legal act with consequences that outlive the competition:

  MIT / Apache-2.0   anyone may use this commercially, including a competitor,
                     with no obligation to share anything back. Apache-2.0 adds
                     an express patent grant, which matters if any of this is
                     ever patented.
  AGPL-3.0           anyone offering this as a network service must publish
                     their changes. The strongest copyleft, and the one most
                     likely to deter a company from adopting it.
  No licence         default copyright applies, nobody may legally use it, and
                     most competitions will reject the submission.

Decide before the first public push, not after. A licence added later does not
retroactively cover what people already took.
